Permanode type:

camliNodeType: "importer"
importerType: "twitter"
authClientID:     "xxx"    // e.g. api token
authClientSecret: "sdkojfsldfjlsdkf"

camliNodeType: "importerAccount"
importerType: "twitter"
twitterAccount: "bradfitz"

Chronological progress and reconciliation
----------------------------------------

When an API's ordering or time filters and its edit semantics permit it,
prefer a stable chronological traversal with a durable, contiguous progress
watermark over repeatedly scanning all history or persisting an offset page
number. Discover the required window, then publish items oldest first.
Advance the watermark only through a prefix whose items have all completed
successfully; the largest timestamp observed or fetched is not a safe
checkpoint.

Use stable source IDs to identify and deduplicate items. Include equality at
the resume boundary: timestamps are not unique, and all items sharing a
timestamp must complete before a timestamp-only watermark advances past
them. Alternatively, use a source-supported stable (timestamp, ID) cursor
with precisely defined boundary semantics. Offset pages can shift when
items are inserted or deleted; they are not durable cursors.

Keep the watermark, its schema/import version, and a separate backfill-done
marker in authoritative account permanode claims. A successful bounded test
or one completed page must not imply that historical backfill is done.
An empty completed history needs a done marker, not a fabricated timestamp,
and must still allow discovery of future items. On a version bump, reuse a
checkpoint only after an explicit compatibility decision; otherwise perform
a safe full reconciliation. Treat unknown-schema payloads as opaque rather
than parsing them with the current format. Missing legacy progress must not
silently skip history, and malformed authoritative progress in the current
schema should produce an error.

Publish item data and its hash/version completion markers atomically after
all uploads succeed. These markers make replay after a crash inexpensive.
Checkpoint the account periodically (for example, every roughly 100 items),
at the end, and on graceful failure when possible, never beyond a failed
item or unfinished timestamp group. Publish related checkpoint attributes
in one atomic account claim. Avoid a cursor claim per item and avoid
rewriting unchanged progress on idle polls.

The discovery strategy must justify the contiguous-prefix guarantee.
Prefer stable source cursors or server-side after/before bounds where
available. Do not infer a global timestamp order from observed pages: without
an API ordering guarantee, enumerate the entire selected range before sorting
and publishing it oldest first. An incomplete or quota-blocked discovery
cannot justify new progress. Keeping that discovery only in memory can prevent
a very large history from ever fitting within a quota window; consider
resumable durable discovery manifests instead. Any manifest required for
correctness belongs in authoritative storage, not a disposable scratch cache.

Offset pagination is not a transactional source snapshot. Deduplicating stable
IDs handles repeated results, but concurrent inserts, deletes, or edits can
still leave unseen items; detect conflicting duplicate summaries and retry,
and document what reconciliation is needed for changes that were not observed.

After backfill, recheck an edit overlap only when the source API's ordering
or filters and update semantics make that useful. State whether its duration
is an API guarantee or merely a local policy. Backdated inserts, edits outside
the window, and changes absent from list summaries may require explicit or
periodic full reconciliation, a modified-since feed, or other source
support. A watermark alone is not complete edit or deletion synchronization.

Keep runtime status separate from data correctness. The host's last-run
start, finish, and error describe the current process's activity; they are
not durable evidence of imported data or a replacement for account and
item claims. Likewise, scratch caches may optimize discovery or quota
tracking but must not become the sole record of import progress.

See strava/README.md for one implementation, including its ordering,
overlap, quota, and reconciliation limitations.

Scratch caches
--------------

RunContext and SetupContext provide two automatically scoped sorted.KeyValue
caches, opened on demand:

- ImporterCache is shared by all accounts of one registered importer.
- AccountCache is private to one importer account, identified by its permanode.

Both methods return (sorted.KeyValue, error). Both scopes can use plain keys
such as "ratelimit" without colliding with other importers, accounts, or the
other scope. Reads, writes, batches, and scans stay in the selected scope.
Close on a cache view returns nil without closing its backing store.

These are disposable scratch caches, NOT authoritative importer state.
Even when persisted, they are not stored, indexed, or replicated in the main
blob storage. A cache may be missing, stale, deleted, or unavailable. An
importer must remain correct without it: cache contents may accelerate work,
but must not be the only record of progress, credentials, or imported data.
Reindexing or restoring blobs does not restore these caches. Strava uses
ImporterCache for its application-wide rate-limit snapshots.

With a configured local blobPath, the server defaults to:

    <blobPath>/importer-cache/strava/cache.sqlite
    <blobPath>/importer-cache/strava-sha224-<account-digest>/cache.sqlite

The importer-wide directory is the registered importer name; the account
directory appends its permanode blobref. Usual lowercase names stay readable;
unusual names are percent-escaped to avoid path and namespace collisions.
The backend follows the file-based index type (sqlite, leveldb, or kv),
defaulting to sorted.DefaultKVFileType when the index has no file-based
backend. LevelDB uses a cache.leveldb directory containing its own files
rather than one SQLite file. Stores and directories are created only when a
cache is first requested, not when the server starts.

Without a local blobPath, the default is memory-only; there is no fallback
to $HOME/.cache. The low-level importer handler can choose a local root and
backend explicitly:

    "handler": "importer",
    "handlerArgs": {
        "cacheRoot": "/srv/perkeep/blobs/importer-cache",
        "cacheType": "sqlite"
    }

cacheType supports sqlite, leveldb, and kv, and defaults to
sorted.DefaultKVFileType. An empty cacheRoot means memory-only. Separate
hosts should use separate roots. Stop the server before deleting stores.

For embedding and tests, HostConfig.CacheRoot and CacheType configure the
same on-demand file stores. HostConfig.Cache can instead inject a backing
sorted.KeyValue whose caller owns its lifetime; it cannot be combined with
CacheRoot or CacheType. Without either option, each importer and account
gets its own memory store, created on demand just like disk stores. Only an
explicitly injected backing store shares storage via prefixed views.

Host.Close cancels managed importer runs and long polls, waiting up to one
second for them to return and owned stores to close. On timeout it returns
an error; background cleanup leaves stores open until managed work returns,
then closes them. Repeated Close calls return the original result, even if
cleanup later finishes. Stop direct users of the Host, including independently
invoked Importer.Run calls, before closing it.
